{"task_id": "HumanEval/75", "solution": "def is_multiply_prime(a):\n    \"\"\"Checks if the given number is the multiplication of 3 prime numbers.\n    Assumes `a` is an integer and `0 < a < 100`.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def generate_primes_up_to(limit):\n        \"\"\"Generates a list of prime numbers up to and including the limit using the Sieve of Eratosthenes.\"\"\"\n        if limit < 2:\n            return []\n        primes = []\n        is_prime_sieve = [True] * (limit + 1)\n        is_prime_sieve[0] = is_prime_sieve[1] = False\n        for p in range(2, limit + 1):\n            if is_prime_sieve[p]:\n                primes.append(p)\n                for multiple in range(p * p, limit + 1, p):\n                    is_prime_sieve[multiple] = False\n        return primes\n\n    # Smallest product of 3 primes is 2 * 2 * 2 = 8.\n    if a < 8:\n        return False\n\n    # Generate primes up to a // 2 + 1. This is a sufficient upper bound for any prime factor.\n    primes = generate_primes_up_to(a // 2 + 1)\n    \n    n = len(primes)\n    # Iterate through all combinations of three primes, allowing repetition.\n    for i in range(n):\n        p1 = primes[i]\n        # Optimization: if p1 * p1 * p1 is already greater than 'a',\n        # any further combinations with larger p1 will also be greater.\n        if p1 * p1 * p1 > a:\n            break\n        \n        for j in range(i, n): # Start from i to allow p1 = p2\n            p2 = primes[j]\n            # Optimization: if p1 * p2 * p2 is already greater than 'a',\n            # any further combinations with larger p2 will also be greater.\n            if p1 * p2 * p2 > a:\n                break\n            \n            for k in range(j, n): # Start from j to allow p2 = p3\n                p3 = primes[k]\n                \n                product = p1 * p2 * p3\n                \n                if product == a:\n                    return True\n                \n                # Optimization: if the current product is already greater than 'a',\n                # any further combinations with larger p3 will also be greater.\n                if product > a:\n                    break\n                    \n    return False"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/80", "solution": "def is_happy(s):\n    \"\"\"Checks if a string is happy.\n    A string is happy if its length is at least 3 and every 3 consecutive letters are distinct.\n    \"\"\"\n    if len(s) < 3:\n        return False\n\n    for i in range(len(s) - 2):\n        if s[i] == s[i+1] or s[i] == s[i+2] or s[i+1] == s[i+2]:\n            return False\n            \n    return True"}
